r  our federal government to plan for every  potential outcome of a u.s  vote this time that planning has more  variables and more  wild cards than usual mercedes stevenson  reports on the extraordinary  preparations  and how canada's response will unfold  [Applause]  unconventional wildly unpredictable  donald trump has shattered our  expectations  of normal and as the 2020 campaign draws  to an  end the president appears willing to  defy the most  basic of democratic norms can you give a  direct answer you will accept the  election  look you i have to see no i'm not going  to just say yes  for months trump has claimed the only  way he can lose  is if there's fraud john bolton  trump's own former national security  adviser  warns the president won't leave  graciously if defeated  if he thinks confusion and chaos  can help him hang on can help affect  recounts and contests  i think there's every prospect he'll  engage in  sources tell global news the canadian  government has been bracing for november  3rd  and the uncertainty that could follow in  the corridors of power across ottawa  planning for this election started early  it has been intense with a surge of  briefings  and intelligence reports preparing for  all scenarios  canada's embassy in washington will be  at the heart of the government's  response  ambassador kirsten hillman has been in  near  daily communication with the prime  minister's office  multiplying her contacts in the white  house congress  the state department and security  agencies like the fbi and cia  so how will canada respond if the result  is  unclear roland paris served as prime  minister trudeau's first  foreign policy and defense advisor he  says  if there's any doubt about who won the  election the prime minister would be  wise to keep his powder dry  canadian prime ministers rarely have  anything to gain  by involving themselves in u.s domestic  politics and they  often have quite a bit to lose aside  from reaffirming its confidence